\section{Fixed effects}{

\itemize{
\item Gaussian within fits: \verb{alpha_i = ybar_i - xbar_i' beta}, the
intercepts of the least-squares dummy-variable regression. They are
not normalized: \code{fixest::fixef()} gives the same values for a model
without an intercept.
\item Poisson within fits: the conditional (Poisson fixed-effects) estimator
does not estimate \code{alpha_i}, but the individual intercept that
maximizes the unconditional likelihood at the conditional \code{beta} has
the closed form \verb{alpha_i = log(sum_t y_it / sum_t exp(x_it' beta))},
the values reported by \code{fixest::fepois()}. Individuals with no
positive outcome (\code{alpha_i = -Inf}) are omitted, as in fixest.
\item Negative binomial and gamma within fits: the dummy-variable
intercepts of the joint maximum likelihood fit. The negative binomial
fit drops the individuals whose outcome is always zero.
\item Two-way fits (Gaussian, Poisson, negative binomial): a list with the
\code{individual} and \code{time} effects, normalized so that the time effects
average zero.
\item The conditional logit (\code{family = "binomial"}, one-way or two-way) and
the fixed-effects ordered logit condition the individual effects out
of the likelihood and do not estimate them: \code{fixef()} stops with an
error. The individual intercepts are not consistently estimable with
a fixed number of periods anyway (incidental parameters).
\item \code{\link[=panhurdle]{panhurdle()}} fits: the dummy-variable intercepts of the zero-truncated
count part (individuals with at least one positive count); the binary
part is a conditional logit.
}
}

\section{Random effects}{

\code{ranef()} returns the posterior mean (\code{type = "mean"}, default) or the
posterior mode (\code{type = "mode"}) of each individual's random effects
given the individual's outcomes, at the estimated parameters (empirical
Bayes).
\itemize{
\item Gaussian random intercept: the BLUP
\verb{sigma_mu^2 T_i / (sigma_mu^2 T_i + sigma_e^2) * mean_t(y_it - x_it' beta)}
with the variance components of the fit (Swamy-Arora, or maximum
likelihood for weighted fits). Mean and mode coincide.
\item Gaussian random slopes: the BLUPs stored by the fit.
\item Binomial, gamma, beta, Tobit and ordered random intercepts, which are
normal on the link scale: the posterior mean is computed by adaptive
Gauss-Hermite quadrature with the \code{R} nodes of the fit, centred at the
posterior mode and scaled by the curvature there, the rule used for the
likelihood. With \code{R = 1} (Laplace) the mean is the mode.
\item Poisson and negative binomial random intercepts (Hausman, Hall and
Griliches), whose mixing distribution is not normal: the effect is
reported on the log scale of the multiplicative effect on the mean.
For the Poisson-gamma model \code{nu_i ~ Gamma(d, d)} and the posterior is
\verb{Gamma(d + sum_t y_it, d + sum_t exp(x_it' beta))}, so the posterior
mean of \code{log(nu_i)} is \code{digamma(d + Y_i) - log(d + L_i)} and its mode
\code{log((d + Y_i) / (d + L_i))}. For the beta-mixed negative binomial
model the effect is \code{log(delta_i)}, with \code{delta_i / (1 + delta_i)}
distributed as \code{Beta(b, a)}, giving \code{digamma(b + Y_i) - digamma(a + L_i)}
and \code{log((b + Y_i) / (a + L_i))}.
\item Random slopes of the Poisson, negative binomial, gamma and binomial
families: posterior modes are those stored by the fit; posterior means
use the adaptive product rule of the fit (\code{R^q} nodes).
}

Empirical Bayes predictions are shrunk towards zero, the more so the
fewer observations an individual has: their spread is smaller than the
estimated standard deviation of the random effect (for the Gaussian
model, \code{var(b_i) = sigma_mu^2 * shrinkage_i}), so their histogram is
narrower than the assumed normal density (see \code{\link[=plot.panglm]{plot.panglm()}},
\code{which = "effects"}).
}

\section{Generics}{

The generics \code{fixef()} and \code{ranef()} are also defined by nlme, whose
generics lme4 and fixest re-export. panglm exports its own generics so
that it does not depend on nlme, and when nlme is loaded it also
registers its methods for nlme's generics: whichever package is
attached last, \code{fixef(fit)} and \code{ranef(fit)} dispatch to the panglm
methods.
}

\examples{
data(copd)
fe <- panglm(fev1 ~ crp, data = copd, index = c("id", "visit"),
             model = "within")
head(fixef(fe))

re <- panglm(fev1 ~ crp + treatment, data = copd,
             index = c("id", "visit"), model = "random")
head(ranef(re))

hosp <- panglm(hospitalized ~ crp + treatment, data = copd,
               index = c("id", "visit"), model = "random",
               family = "binomial")
head(ranef(hosp))
}
